 Interactive debugger for the UEFI dev console. Memory inspector,
 port I/O probe, breakpoint table, stack walker, and perf counters.
 Operates on the live system -- no GDB, no host-side tooling.

 We say:

Section: Debugger Mode

  DebugMode =
    | DebugMemory
    | DebugIO
    | DebugBreakpoints
    | DebugStack
    | DebugPerf
    | DebugRegisters
    | DebugDisk
    | DebugReport

Section: Breakpoint

  Breakpoint = record {
    id : Integer,
    address : Integer,
    original-byte : Integer,
    enabled : Boolean,
    hit-count : Integer,
    label : Text
  }

  BreakpointTable = record {
    entries : List Breakpoint,
    next-id : Integer
  }

  bp-table-empty : BreakpointTable = BreakpointTable { entries = [], next-id = 1 }

  bp-add : BreakpointTable, Integer, Text -> BreakpointTable
  bp-add (tbl) (addr) (label) =
    let orig = peek-byte addr 0
    in let bp = Breakpoint { id = tbl.next-id, address = addr, original-byte = orig, enabled = True, hit-count = 0, label = label }
    in let w = poke-byte addr 0 204
    in BreakpointTable { entries = list-snoc (tbl.entries) bp, next-id = tbl.next-id + 1 }

  bp-remove : BreakpointTable, Integer -> BreakpointTable
  bp-remove (tbl) (id) =
    BreakpointTable { entries = bp-filter (tbl.entries) id 0 (list-length (tbl.entries)) [], next-id = tbl.next-id }

  bp-filter : List Breakpoint, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Breakpoint -> List Breakpoint
  bp-filter (bps) (remove-id) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let bp = list-at bps i
    in if bp.id == remove-id then
      let w = poke-byte (bp.address) 0 (bp.original-byte)
      in bp-filter bps remove-id (i + 1) len acc
    else bp-filter bps remove-id (i + 1) len (list-push acc bp)

Section: Debugger State

 `watch-ports` and `dev` are different things and the names are close
 enough to be worth separating here. `watch-ports` is the I/O port probe
 list; `dev` holds the MEMORY watch table from `Dev chapter DevState`,
 which is what `dbg watch` in the design means.

 `last-address` stays the one home for the cursor and for what `$` reads.
 The design's `DevState` record also lists a `last-result`; putting one
 there would have made two fields holding the same number with nothing
 checking they agree, so DevState carries the watch table and nothing
 else. That reasoning is written out in the chapter itself.

 `pending` is what lets a command need a SECOND word. The console
 dispatches one line at a time, so a command that takes an argument has to
 say it is waiting and the next line has to know it was expected. Before
 this, `Memory Write` set a status that said "enter address value" and
 nothing anywhere consumed the next line: it fell through to the address
 evaluator, moved the cursor, and no write ever happened. The menu offered
 a command the console could not perform.

 A read acts at the cursor and answers immediately, the way `watch` does,
 because the cursor is already how every address in this console is chosen
 -- an expression moves it, and the command acts there. That keeps one
 idiom instead of two.

 THE THREE PENDING CASES DO NOT CONSUME THEIR LINE THE SAME WAY, and that
 is the reason this is a variant rather than a Boolean. A write value is an
 EXPRESSION and goes through `ExprEval`, so `0x2a` and `$ - 1` both work.
 A symbol name and a breakpoint name are TEXT and must not: `main` is a
 name, and an evaluator handed it would answer "unknown name" for
 something that was never meant to be one. What the flag records is not
 "a line is expected" but "which reader gets it".

  DebugPending =
   | PendingNone
   | PendingWriteValue
   | PendingSymName
   | PendingBpName
   | PendingSearchPattern
   | PendingCompareAddr
   | PendingDiskLba
   | PendingDiskCmp
   | PendingIdtVec
   | PendingMacroName
   | PendingMacroBody
   | PendingMacroRun
   | PendingRepeat
   | PendingCpuLeaf
   | PendingPortRead
   | PendingPortWatch

 THE DISK CURSOR IS SEPARATE FROM `last-address`, and it has to be. One is
 an address in the flat physical space and the other is a sector number on
 a medium; a single cursor meaning both would read the same number two
 ways depending on which command was asked, which is the kind of thing
 that is only noticed after it has dumped the wrong thing.

 `disk-lines` HOLDS A RENDERED REPORT RATHER THAN THE VIEW RE-READING IT.
 The memory dump can be produced during rendering because `peek-byte`
 costs nothing, but a sector read is a disk transaction and 512 bytes of
 heap that is never returned, so a view that produced one would perform
 I/O and leak a buffer on every repaint. The disk commands read once, when
 the operator asks, and store what they found.

  DebuggerState = record {
    mode : DebugMode,
    breakpoints : BreakpointTable,
    last-address : Integer,
    dump-length : Integer,
    watch-ports : List Integer,
    status : Text,
    map1 : Maybe Map1Info,
    dev : DevState,
    pending : DebugPending,
    last-lba : Integer,
    disk-lines : List Text,
    last-vector : Integer,
    report-lines : List Text,
    serial-mirror : Boolean,
    serial-remote : Boolean,
    serial-line : Text,
    macros : MacroTable,
    macro-running : Boolean,
    macro-name : Text,
    last-leaf : Integer,
    port-trace : PortTrace
  }

 A FOURTH CURSOR, and the argument is the one the other three made. An
 address, a sector, an interrupt vector and a CPUID leaf are four
 different spaces, and a number from any one of them is meaningless in
 the others; a single cursor would read the same number four ways
 depending on which command asked. The leaf cursor earns its place the
 same way the vector cursor did: `$ + 1` steps to the next leaf, which is
 how anyone enumerates CPUID.

 `macro-running` IS THE WHOLE RECURSION BOUND. A macro body that runs a
 macro can reach itself, and under no collector and no stack guard that is
 a hang. `macro.run` is refused outright while this is set, rather than
 bounded by a depth counter: a flag is checkable by reading it, a depth
 limit has to be got right and then trusted, and nothing a debugger macro
 is for needs nesting.

 `macro-name` carries the name from the first prompt to the second, since
 defining a macro is the one command here that needs two lines of input.
 It is not a cursor and nothing reads it outside that pair.

 THE TWO SERIAL FLAGS ARE INDEPENDENT, and the design's command table
 reads as though they are one mode with two positions (`serial.remote`
 against `serial.local`). They are not, because they govern opposite
 directions: mirroring is what the console SAYS on COM1 and remote is what
 it LISTENS to there, and wanting the first without the second is the
 ordinary case -- watching a console you are driving by keyboard.

 `serial-remote` NEVER DISABLES THE KEYBOARD. The poll checks the serial
 line in addition to `uefi-con-poll-key`, never instead of it, so the flag
 cannot strand an operator in front of a console that has stopped
 listening to them. That is a deliberate divergence from the `serial.local`
 row, which reads as a switch back from a mode this does not enter.

 `serial-line` is the PARTIAL line: bytes arrive one poll at a time and a
 command is not a command until its terminator does.

 A THIRD CURSOR, for the same reason there was a second. `last-address` is
 an address in the flat physical space and `last-lba` is a sector on a
 medium; a vector is an index into a 256-entry table, and it is the one of
 the three where a number from either of the others is guaranteed
 meaningless. `report-lines` is a rendered report for consistency with the
 disk view rather than out of necessity: every read behind it is a `peek`
 and costs nothing, so this one could safely be produced during rendering.
 Two views built the same way is worth more here than one saved field.

  debugger-init : DebuggerState
  debugger-init = DebuggerState {
    mode = DebugMemory,
    breakpoints = bp-table-empty,
    last-address = 1048576,
    dump-length = 256,
    watch-ports = [],
    status = "Debugger ready",
    map1 = None,
    dev = dev-state-new,
    pending = PendingNone,
    last-lba = 0,
    disk-lines = [],
    last-vector = 0,
    report-lines = [],
    serial-mirror = False,
    serial-remote = False,
    serial-line = "",
    macros = macro-table-new,
    macro-running = False,
    macro-name = "",
    last-leaf = 0,
    port-trace = pt-new
  }

