Section: Reading And Writing One Cell

 `Dev chapter MemInspector` already reads and writes memory, and these do
 not call it, which is worth a sentence so nobody deletes one as a
 duplicate. Every MemInspector entry point takes and returns a
 `ConsoleState` from `Kernel chapter Console` -- it writes its answer to
 the kernel console as a side effect. This console is a `DevConsoleState`
 drawn through UEFI text-out, and it shows a command's answer by putting
 it in `status`, which is a `Text`. So what is needed here is the same
 arithmetic returning a value rather than performing an output.

 These are PURE. `peek-byte` and `poke-byte` carry no effect row, so the
 whole of the read and the whole of the write are ordinary functions, and
 the console's use of them can be tested without a machine.

  debug-read-u16 : Integer -> Integer
  debug-read-u16 (a) = bit-or (peek-byte a 0) (bit-shl (peek-byte a 1) 8)

  debug-read-u32 : Integer -> Integer
  debug-read-u32 (a) =
    let b2 = bit-shl (peek-byte a 2) 16
    in let b3 = bit-shl (peek-byte a 3) 24
    in bit-or (debug-read-u16 a) (bit-or b2 b3)

  debug-read-u64 : Integer -> Integer
  debug-read-u64 (a) =
    let h1 = bit-shl (peek-byte a 5) 8
    in let h2 = bit-shl (peek-byte a 6) 16
    in let h3 = bit-shl (peek-byte a 7) 24
    in let hi = bit-or (peek-byte a 4) (bit-or h1 (bit-or h2 h3))
    in bit-or (debug-read-u32 a) (bit-shl hi 32)

 All four widths on one line, because the menu item promises all four and
 because the question an operator has at an address is usually "which of
 these is it" rather than one width chosen in advance.

  debug-mem-read-line : Integer -> Text
  debug-mem-read-line (addr) =
    let hb = "  b=#" & hex-byte (peek-byte addr 0)
    in let hw = "  w=#" & hex-i32 (debug-read-u16 addr)
    in let hd = "  d=#" & hex-i32 (debug-read-u32 addr)
    in let hq = "  q=#" & hex-i64 (debug-read-u64 addr)
    in "0x" & hex-i64 addr & hb & hw & hd & hq

 A value outside a byte is REFUSED and nothing is written. `poke-byte`
 would take the low eight bits and the operator would be told a byte had
 been stored, which is a different byte from the one they typed. The
 answer names the value it rejected so the refusal is not mistaken for a
 write that happened to change nothing.

  debug-mem-write-at : Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-mem-write-at (addr) (val) =
    if val < 0 then "refused: " & show val & " is not a byte (0..255), nothing written"
    else if val > 255 then "refused: " & show val & " is not a byte (0..255), nothing written"
    else let old = peek-byte addr 0
    in let w = poke-byte addr 0 val
    in "0x" & hex-i64 addr & ": #" & hex-byte old & " -> #" & hex-byte val

Section: Searching And Comparing

 These are the last two commands the design specifies and the chapter did
 not have. The specification writes them as `dbg mem.s <addr> <bytes>` and
 `dbg mem.cmp <a1> <a2> <len>`, which read as commands needing two and
 three arguments, and this console dispatches one line at a time. That
 looked like a reason to build a multi-argument parser.

 IT IS NOT, BECAUSE THE CONSOLE ALREADY ANSWERS THE QUESTION. Every
 address here is chosen by moving the cursor with an expression, and every
 command acts there; `watch` additionally takes its length from the dump
 length, so the region it watches is the region `mem` would have shown.
 Applying the same two rules leaves each command needing exactly ONE
 argument: a search is a pattern at the cursor, and a compare is the
 cursor against one other address. The parser was not needed.

 THE PATTERN IS PARSED WITH `hex-char-val` FROM `Dev chapter HexFormat`
 rather than a fourth spelling of the same table. That function is already
 CCE-correct in the way this project keeps having to relearn: digits are
 contiguous in CCE so the digit arm may do arithmetic, letters are ordered
 by English frequency so the letter arm is a six-way comparison. Writing
 `10 + c - 'a'` here would read `0xff` as 391, which is exactly what
 ExprEval shipped and had to be repaired.

  debug-pattern-len : Text -> Integer
  debug-pattern-len (t) = text-length t / 2

  debug-pattern-nibble : Text, Integer -> Integer
  debug-pattern-nibble (t) (i) = hex-char-val (char-code (char-at t i))

  debug-pattern-byte : Text, Integer -> Integer
  debug-pattern-byte (t) (b) =
    let hi = debug-pattern-nibble t (b * 2)
    in let lo = debug-pattern-nibble t (b * 2 + 1)
    in if hi < 0 then 0 - 1
    else if lo < 0 then 0 - 1
    else hi * 16 + lo

 A pattern is refused rather than truncated. An odd number of characters
 is a typo, not half a byte, and a non-hex character is a typo too; either
 one silently dropped would search for something the operator did not ask
 for and report an address they would then trust.

  debug-pattern-fault : Text -> Text
  debug-pattern-fault (t) =
    if text-length t == 0 then "empty pattern"
    else if int-rem (text-length t) 2 /= 0 then "pattern needs an even number of hex digits"
    else debug-pattern-scan t 0 (debug-pattern-len t)

  debug-pattern-scan : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-pattern-scan (t) (b) (n) =
    if b >= n then ""
    else if debug-pattern-byte t b < 0 then "not hex: " & t
    else debug-pattern-scan t (b + 1) n

  debug-pattern-at : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  debug-pattern-at (t) (n) (addr) (off) =
    if n <= 0 then True
    else if peek-byte addr off /= debug-pattern-byte t 0 then False
    else debug-pattern-rest t n addr off 1

  debug-pattern-rest : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  debug-pattern-rest (t) (n) (addr) (off) (b) =
    if b >= n then True
    else if peek-byte addr (off + b) /= debug-pattern-byte t b then False
    else debug-pattern-rest t n addr off (b + 1)

 The window is the dump length, and the answer names the ADDRESS as well
 as the offset. An offset alone is a number the operator has to add to
 something, and the thing they would add it to is the cursor they are
 about to move.

  debug-mem-search : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  debug-mem-search (addr) (len) (pat) =
    let fault = debug-pattern-fault pat
    in if text-length fault > 0 then "refused: " & fault
    else debug-search-loop pat (debug-pattern-len pat) addr len 0

  debug-search-loop : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-search-loop (pat) (n) (addr) (len) (off) =
    if off + n > len then "not found in " & show len & " bytes from 0x" & hex-i64 addr
    else if debug-pattern-at pat n addr off then debug-search-hit addr off
    else debug-search-loop pat n addr len (off + 1)

  debug-search-hit : Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-search-hit (addr) (off) =
    "found at 0x" & hex-i64 (addr + off) & " (cursor + " & show off & ")"

 A compare reports the FIRST difference and both bytes, not a count. A
 count says two regions differ and leaves the operator to find where; the
 offset is the thing they were going to look for next.

  debug-mem-compare : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-mem-compare (a) (b) (len) = debug-compare-loop a b len 0

  debug-compare-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-compare-loop (a) (b) (len) (i) =
    if i >= len then "identical over " & show len & " bytes"
    else if peek-byte a i /= peek-byte b i then debug-compare-diff a b i
    else debug-compare-loop a b len (i + 1)

  debug-compare-diff : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  debug-compare-diff (a) (b) (i) =
    let av = "#" & hex-byte (peek-byte a i)
    in let bv = "#" & hex-byte (peek-byte b i)
    in "differ at +" & show i & ": 0x" & hex-i64 (a + i) & "=" & av & " 0x" & hex-i64 (b + i) & "=" & bv

Section: Memory Commands

  debug-mem-dump : DebuggerState, Integer, Integer -> List Text
  debug-mem-dump (dbg) (addr) (len) =
    debug-dump-lines addr len 0 []

  debug-dump-lines : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  debug-dump-lines (addr) (len) (off) (acc) =
    if off >= len then acc
    else let line-bytes = if len - off >= 16 then 16 else len - off
    in let hex-part = dump-hex-bytes addr off line-bytes 0 ""
    in let ascii-part = dump-ascii-bytes addr off line-bytes 0 ""
    in let line = hex-i64 (addr + off) & "  " & hex-part & " |" & ascii-part & "|"
    in debug-dump-lines addr len (off + 16) (list-push acc line)

  dump-hex-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  dump-hex-bytes (base) (off) (count) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 16 then acc
    else if i >= count then dump-hex-bytes base off count (i + 1) (acc & "   ")
    else let b = peek-byte base (off + i)
    in let sep = if i == 8 then "  " else " "
    in dump-hex-bytes base off count (i + 1) (acc & hex-byte b & sep)

  dump-ascii-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  dump-ascii-bytes (base) (off) (count) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= count then acc
    else let b = peek-byte base (off + i)
    in let ch = if b >= 32 then if b < 127 then char-to-text (code-to-char b) else "." else "."
    in dump-ascii-bytes base off count (i + 1) (acc & ch)

Section: Symbol Lookup

  debug-sym-lookup : SourceIndex, Text -> Text
  debug-sym-lookup (idx) (query) =
    let results = search-by-prefix idx query
    in if list-length results == 0 then "No symbols matching '" & query & "'"
    else format-search-results results

Section: MAP1 Debug Map

  Map1Info = record {
    base : Integer,
    count : Integer,
    strings-base : Integer
  }

 The emitter writes the magic as the four bytes `[77, 65, 80, 49]`, which
 is "MAP1" (`map1-magic` in `codex/compiler/Emit/X86_64Chapter.codex`).
 Read back little-endian that is 0x3150414D, and this constant is what
 `peek-32` is compared against.

 It was 826361677 until 2026-07-28, which is 0x3141474D, or "MGA1". No
 emitted binary has ever contained those bytes, so `map1-find` answered
 None every time it was called, in every binary, since it was written.

  map1-magic-le : Integer = 827343181

  map1-find : Maybe Map1Info
  map1-find =
    map1-scan 1048576 5242880

 THE STEP IS ONE BYTE AND IT HAS TO BE. This scanned by four until
 2026-07-28, on the assumption that the map is four-byte aligned, and it
 is not: the emitter appends MAP1 wherever the preceding section ends.
 Measured in three separately compiled binaries, the magic landed at an
 address congruent to 3 modulo 4 every time, which a scan stepping by
 four from an aligned base can never read. Correcting the magic alone
 left this answering None, which is how the alignment came to light.

 The cost is a byte-wise walk of the search window on the FIRST call
 only. `map1` in `DebuggerState` caches the result, and every caller
 already consults that field before calling here.

  map1-scan : Integer, Integer -> Maybe Map1Info
  map1-scan (addr) (limit) =
    if addr >= limit then None
    else let word = peek-32 addr 0
    in if word == map1-magic-le then map1-scan-check addr limit
    else map1-scan (addr + 1) limit

  map1-scan-check : Integer, Integer -> Maybe Map1Info
  map1-scan-check (addr) (limit) =
    when map1-validate addr
      is Just (info) -> Just info
      is None -> map1-scan (addr + 1) limit

  map1-validate : Integer -> Maybe Map1Info
  map1-validate (addr) =
    let count = peek-32 addr 4
    in let str-off = peek-32 addr 8
    in if count > 0 then if count < 100000 then if str-off == 12 + count * 12
       then Just (Map1Info { base = addr, count = count, strings-base = addr + str-off })
       else None
       else None
    else None

  Map1Entry = record {
    code-offset : Integer,
    code-size : Integer,
    name-offset : Integer
  }

  map1-read-entry : Integer, Integer -> Map1Entry
  map1-read-entry (base) (i) =
    let addr = base + 12 + i * 12
    in Map1Entry { code-offset = peek-32 addr 0, code-size = peek-32 addr 4, name-offset = peek-32 addr 8 }

  map1-lookup : Map1Info, Integer -> Maybe Map1Entry
  map1-lookup (info) (target) =
    map1-bsearch info target 0 (info.count)

  map1-bsearch : Map1Info, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Maybe Map1Entry
  map1-bsearch (info) (target) (lo) (hi) =
    if lo >= hi then None
    else let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
    in let entry = map1-read-entry (info.base) mid
    in if target < entry.code-offset then map1-bsearch info target lo mid
    else if target >= entry.code-offset + entry.code-size then map1-bsearch info target (mid + 1) hi
    else Just entry

  map1-read-name : Map1Info, Integer -> Text
  map1-read-name (info) (name-off) =
    map1-read-chars (info.strings-base + name-off) 0 ""

  map1-read-chars : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  map1-read-chars (addr) (i) (acc) =
    let b = peek-byte addr i
    in if b == 0 then acc
    else if i > 200 then acc
    else map1-read-chars addr (i + 1) (acc & char-to-text (code-to-char b))

  resolve-addr : DebuggerState, Integer -> Text
  resolve-addr (dbg) (rip) =
    let info = when (dbg.map1)
      is Just (m) -> Just m
      is None -> map1-find
    in when info
      is None -> "<no debug info>"
      is Just (m) ->
        let target = rip - 1048576
        in when (map1-lookup m target)
          is None -> "<unknown 0x" & hex-i64 rip & ">"
          is Just (entry) ->
            let name = map1-read-name m (entry.name-offset)
            in let off = target - entry.code-offset
            in name & "+0x" & hex-i32 off

Section: Symbolic Breakpoints

  map1-find-by-name : Map1Info, Text -> Maybe Map1Entry
  map1-find-by-name (info) (name) =
    map1-name-scan info name 0 (info.count)

  map1-name-scan : Map1Info, Text, Integer, Integer -> Maybe Map1Entry
  map1-name-scan (info) (name) (i) (count) =
    if i >= count then None
    else let entry = map1-read-entry (info.base) i
    in let entry-name = map1-read-name info (entry.name-offset)
    in if entry-name == name then Just entry
    else map1-name-scan info name (i + 1) count

  bp-add-symbolic : DebuggerState, Text -> DebuggerState
  bp-add-symbolic (dbg) (name) =
    let info = when (dbg.map1)
      is Just (m) -> Just m
      is None -> map1-find
    in let dbg1 = when info is Just (m) -> __record-set dbg "map1" (Just m) is None -> dbg
    in when info
      is None -> __record-set dbg1 "status" "No debug map found"
      is Just (m) ->
        when map1-find-by-name m name
          is None -> __record-set dbg1 "status" ("Symbol not found: " & name)
          is Just (entry) ->
            let addr = 1048576 + entry.code-offset
            in let tbl = bp-add (dbg1.breakpoints) addr name
            in let d2 = __record-set dbg1 "breakpoints" tbl
            in __record-set d2 "status" ("Breakpoint #" & show (tbl.next-id - 1) & " at " & name & " (0x" & hex-i64 addr & ")")

  bp-list-text : BreakpointTable -> List Text
  bp-list-text (tbl) =
    if list-length (tbl.entries) == 0 then ["  (no breakpoints)"]
    else bp-list-format (tbl.entries) 0 (list-length (tbl.entries)) []

  bp-list-format : List Breakpoint, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  bp-list-format (bps) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let bp = list-at bps i
    in let en = if bp.enabled then "ON " else "OFF"
    in let line = "  #" & show (bp.id) & " " & en & " 0x" & hex-i64 (bp.address) & " hits=" & show (bp.hit-count) & " " & bp.label
    in bp-list-format bps (i + 1) len (list-push acc line)

Section: Where A RIP Actually Comes From

 THIS SECTION USED TO READ THREE UNRELATED CELLS AND CALL THEM CPU STATE.
 It declared an exception record at 28928 with a vector, a RIP and an RSP
 at the first three offsets. There is no exception record on this machine
 and there never was: 28928 is `stdin-eof-settled-addr`, and the two cells
 the RIP and RSP were taken from are `cap-expiry-addr` and
 `sched-ready-head-addr`. So the Registers, Stack and Backtrace views were
 rendering the standard-input EOF flag as a fault vector and walking a
 stack from whatever the scheduler's ready-list head happened to hold.

 It is the shape this project keeps meeting. Nothing was ever blank or
 obviously wrong, so three menu items showed confident numbers and looked
 like they worked. The address map in `X86_64Boot` is the referee, and it
 records no faulting vector anywhere.

 WHAT THE MACHINE DOES KEEP is the watchdog's ring: the timer ISR writes a
 RIP, an RSP and a heap pointer into it when neither the heap pointer nor
 the saved RIP has moved since the last tick. Those are real registers,
 sampled at the moment worth looking at. They are NOT a trap frame, so
 nothing below calls them one and no line reports a vector, because there
 is no vector to report.

Section: Backtrace

  debug-backtrace : DebuggerState, Integer, Integer -> List Text
  debug-backtrace (dbg) (rsp) (max-frames) =
    debug-bt-walk dbg rsp max-frames 0 []

  debug-bt-walk : DebuggerState, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  debug-bt-walk (dbg) (rsp) (max) (depth) (acc) =
    if depth >= max then acc
    else if rsp == 0 then acc
    else if rsp >= 2147483648 then acc
    else let ret-addr = peek-qword rsp 0
    in if ret-addr == 0 then acc
    else let sym = resolve-addr dbg ret-addr
    in let line = "  #" & show depth & " 0x" & hex-i64 ret-addr & " " & sym
    in debug-bt-walk dbg (rsp + 8) max (depth + 1) (list-push acc line)

  debug-backtrace-from-stall : DebuggerState -> List Text
  debug-backtrace-from-stall (dbg) =
    if ii-stall-sampled then debug-backtrace-walk-stall dbg
    else ["  No stall sample has been taken, so there is no stack to walk.",
          "  This machine keeps no exception record; the watchdog ring is",
          "  the only place a RIP is ever stored."]

  debug-backtrace-walk-stall : DebuggerState -> List Text
  debug-backtrace-walk-stall (dbg) =
    let rip = ii-stall-rip
    in let header = "  => 0x" & hex-i64 rip & " " & resolve-addr dbg rip
    in ["  Newest watchdog stall sample:", header] & debug-backtrace dbg ii-stall-rsp 16

Section: Register View

  debug-register-view : List Text
  debug-register-view =
    if ii-stall-sampled then
      ["  Newest watchdog stall sample (no exception record is kept):",
       "  RIP = 0x" & hex-i64 ii-stall-rip,
       "  RSP = 0x" & hex-i64 ii-stall-rsp]
    else ["  No registers to show.",
          "  The watchdog has taken no stall sample since boot, and this",
          "  machine records no faulting vector, RIP or RSP anywhere."]

Section: Perf View

  debug-perf-view : List Text
  debug-perf-view =
    let hpet-lo = peek-32 4275830784 240
    in let hpet-hi = peek-32 4275830784 244
    in ["  Performance Counters:",
        "  HPET counter = 0x" & hex-i32 hpet-hi & hex-i32 hpet-lo]

Section: Port Watch

 THE WATCH LIST HAD NO WAY TO GROW UNTIL `io.watch` LANDED. `watch-ports`
 was initialised empty and no command anywhere added to it, so the Port
 I/O view rendered an empty list on every repaint since it was written --
 a menu item that drew a heading and nothing under it, which reads as a
 machine with nothing to say rather than as a view with no input.

 The cap is here rather than in the view because the view is a repaint:
 every port in this list is read again on every frame, so the list's
 length is a per-frame I/O cost and not merely storage.

  debug-max-watch-ports : Integer = 16

  debug-port-watch : List Integer -> [Device.Port] List Text
  debug-port-watch (ports) = debug-port-watch-loop ports 0 (list-length ports) []

  debug-port-watch-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Text -> [Device.Port] List Text
  debug-port-watch-loop (ports) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else let port = list-at ports i
    in act
      val <- port-in-byte port
      let line = "port 0x" & hex-i32 port & " = 0x" & hex-byte val & " (" & show val & ")"
      in debug-port-watch-loop ports (i + 1) len (list-push acc line)
    end

